            {
              "id": "a0dd57f",
              "hide": [],
              "cls": [
                "FAQ"
              ],
              "type": "accordion",
              "items": [
                {
                  "title": "Do I really need a Will if I’m still young and healthy?",
                  "html": "Yes. Having a Will ensures your children, spouse, and assets are protected if the unexpected happens. Without one, Ontario’s intestacy laws decide who inherits, which may not reflect your wishes."
                },
                {
                  "title": "What happens to my children if I don’t name a guardian in my Will?",
                  "html": "If no guardian is legally appointed, the courts decide who cares for your children. A Will lets you choose a trusted guardian and prevents family disputes."
                },
                {
                  "title": "Can my spouse automatically make decisions for me if I become incapacitated?",
                  "html": "Not necessarily. Without a Power of Attorney, your spouse may need court approval. Setting up POAs ensures your spouse or chosen person can manage finances and healthcare seamlessly."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
